why do covet variants keep getting more  transmissible this chart shows how  infectious different versions are  compared to the original strain of the  covet 19 virus look how much better  omicron and its successors are at  spreading in relation to variants  earlier in the pandemic  two main factors are giving the virus an  advantage in its race to infect how  contagious it is and whether our own  defenses tip the scales we'll also go  over what all that means for our  prospect of getting off the covet  variant treadmill  let's start with how much better the  virus is getting at infecting and  spreading one of the most important  factors is its innate contagiousness  during the pandemic variants of gain  mutations that have made it easier for  them to spread among people some have  helped the variants bind to cells and  then enter them many of these changes  have sprung up in the spike protein a  structure on the surface of the virus  that helps it attach to and enter our  cells earlier in the pandemic that alone  could give a variant a significant  advantage the better a variant is at  attaching the more likely it will enter  the cell to make copies of itself but as  we've developed antibodies and other  immune defenses that's put pressure on  the virus to evolve and mutate in ways  that allow it to evade antibodies  which brings us to immune evasion so  let's go back to that chart again  omicron was roughly six times more  transmissible than the original strain  of coping it had more than 50 mutations  with about 30 in its spike protein alone  some of those that made it easier for  omicron to get into cells but other  mutations made it more difficult for  antibodies to bind to the virus which is  what keeps it from getting into cells  that viral combination especially immune  evasion is what made omicron more  transmissible and that's what drove last  winter's spike in infections if a  variant is more effective at spreading  either because of increased  infectiousness better immune evasion or  both it can outcompete other variants  and become the dominant one again that's  what happened with omicron and then with  ba2 which was almost nine times as  transmissible as the original strain of  covet that's to be expected that's what  viruses do they hone their genetics to  become better at spreading for instance  some studies suggest that ba5 one of the  latest omicron subvariants has mutated  further to evade antibodies increasing  its chance of escaping them  so does that mean we're doomed no  scientists say they do expect to  continue to see variants that are more  transmissible and that outcompete  previous versions of the virus and more  cases can mean more deaths some experts  think there are limits to how much the  virus can mutate because too many  changes could actually prevent it from  getting into cells it's kind of like if  you bend a key too much it won't fit in  its lock anymore but how good of an  immunivator it can become is a different  story as new variants infect us we gain  immunity plus our prior immune defenses  can still protect us but to a lower  degree vaccine makers are also making  updated shots that are tailored to new  variants in august the uk became the  first country to approve a coveted  vaccine targeting omicron and other  countries like the us are likely to  follow we're probably going to see more  vaccine updates as we continue to  coexist with the virus if you're  interested in just how infectious  omicron is and how it changed the course  of the pandemic check out this video i  made and see you next time  [Music]
 

